@page "/utility-text"

<CatHead Title="Text utilities">
    How one line reads: where it sits, how loud it is, and what happens when it is longer than the box
    it is in. These are the utilities without the <code>sedna-</code> prefix, because
    <code>.text-end</code> means the same thing everywhere.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityText.Alignment" Title="Alignment and numbers" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.text-start</code>, <code>.text-center</code> and <code>.text-end</code> are logical, so
        they mirror with the document direction. <code>.text-nums</code> is tabular figures — every
        digit the same width, so a column lines up and a counter does not jitter — and
        <code>.text-nowrap</code> keeps a label on one line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityText.Tone" Title="Size and tone" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.text-sm</code> and <code>.text-lg</code> are two type steps either side of the body
        size, for a line that has to be quieter or louder without becoming a heading.
        <code>.text-muted</code> is the secondary foreground and <code>.text-mono</code> the monospace
        stack — an id, a key, a path. <code>.eyebrow</code> is the small spaced label above a heading
        that says which section or kind it belongs to; it has no margin, so it sits on the heading.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityText.Overflow" Title="Overflow" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.text-truncate</code> is one line cut with an ellipsis, and it includes
        <code>min-width: 0</code> because the usual parent is a flex item that otherwise refuses to
        shrink below its content — the most common reason truncation “does not work”.
        <code>.text-clamp-2</code> and <code>.text-clamp-3</code> cut after two or three lines, and
        <code>.text-break</code> breaks inside a word when there is nowhere else to break.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityText.ShowMore" Title="Show more" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> A button with <code>data-clamp-toggle</code> and <code>aria-controls</code>
        naming a <code>.text-clamp-2</code> or <code>.text-clamp-3</code> block opens it in full and closes it
        again: the script flips the button's <code>aria-expanded</code> and writes
        <code>data-clamp-open</code> on the block, which lifts the clamp. Both labels are the app's &mdash;
        <code>.text-clamp-more</code> shows while it is closed and <code>.text-clamp-less</code> while it is
        open. Where the text fits inside the clamp, as in the second note, the button carries
        <code>data-clamp-fits</code> and is not shown; that is measured again whenever the block changes size.
        <code>sednaUi.clamp.toggle(button, open?)</code> does the same from script.
    </p>
</CatExample>
